      "text": "To solve the expression \\(1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\), we first need to identify the pattern in the sequence. The sequence alternates between positive and negative terms, starting with a positive term and followed by a negative term. The terms themselves form an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and common difference \\(d = 3\\).\n\nThe general term of the sequence can be written as:\n\\[a_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 = 3n - 2\\]\n\nWe need to find the number of terms in the sequence. The last term is 55, so we set up the equation:\n\\[3n - 2 = 55\\]\n\\[3n = 57\\]\n\\[n = 19\\]\nSo, there are 19 terms in the sequence.\n\nNow, we need to group the terms in pairs:\n\\[(1 - 4) + (7 - 10) + (13 - 16) + \\cdots + (49 - 52) + 55\\]\n\nEach pair \\(a_{2k-1} + a_{2k}\\) simplifies to:\n\\[a_{2k-1} = 3(2k-1) - 2 = 6k - 5\\]\n\\[a_{2k} = 3(2k) - 2 = 6k - 2\\]\nSo,\n\\[a_{2k-1} + a_{2k} = (6k - 5) + (6k - 2) = 12k - 7\\]\n\nWe need to find the number of pairs. Since there are 19 terms, there are 9 pairs and one remaining term (55). The sum of the pairs is:\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{9} (12k - 7) = \\sum_{k=1}^{9} 12k - \\sum_{k=1}^{9} 7 = 12 \\sum_{k=1}^{9} k - 7 \\cdot 9\\]\nThe sum of the first 9 positive integers is:\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{9} k = \\frac{9 \\cdot 10}{2} = 45\\]\nSo,\n\\[12 \\sum_{k=1}^{9} k = 12 \\cdot 45 = 540\\]\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{9} (12k - 7) = 540 - 63 = 477\\]\nAdding the remaining term 55:\n\\[477 + 55 = 532\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is:\n\\[\\boxed{532}\\]",
